sers/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commit/037a9013295204cda7b4d046c49f949814ecc945","node_id":"C_kwDOTnPgT9oAKDAzN2E5MDEzMjk1MjA0Y2RhN2I0ZDA0NmM0OWY5NDk4MTRlY2M5NDU","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commit/0520b5576f8a4a227fc79c0b04fe4cba87f5fa2c","sha":"0520b5576f8a4a227fc79c0b04fe4cba87f5fa2c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commits/0520b5576f8a4a227fc79c0b04fe4cba87f5fa2c"}],"sha":"037a9013295204cda7b4d046c49f949814ecc945","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commits/037a9013295204cda7b4d046c49f949814ecc945"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/13798190?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/JuanMelendres","id":13798190,"login":"JuanMelendres","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjEzNzk4MTkw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commits/0520b5576f8a4a227fc79c0b04fe4cba87f5fa2c/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-30T19:23:45Z","email":"juan.mele97@gmail.com","name":"Juan Antonio Melendres Villa"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-30T19:23:45Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat: add Java concurrency fundamentals on-ramp (T-2214) (#199)\n\nThird and final item from the queue following the user''s lost interview, where\nconcurrency was one of the topics they knew but could not explain.\n\nThe audit result was mixed rather than flattering. The concepts were already\ncovered -- the Phase 5 L1/L2 retrofit gave the existing chapters genuine\nplain-language Foundation sections, and claiming the material was missing would\nhave been wrong. Two things were genuinely absent, both verified by count:\n02-java/concurrency was the only Java subdomain without a Junior Fundamentals\non-ramp, and `new Thread(` had zero occurrences anywhere in 02-java. The\nrepository never showed how to start a thread.\n\nNew chapter assembles what fifteen Senior/Staff chapters cover separately and\nhands off to each by name. Measured, pure JDK, OpenJDK 21.0.12, 10 cores:\n\n- 8 threads x 100,000 increments on a shared int produced 229,765 of 800,000 --\n  roughly 70-85% lost across six runs, with nothing thrown.\n- The same workload with a volatile counter produced 177,394. Across six runs\n  the plain range (132,587-229,765) and volatile range (135,457-195,568)\n  overlapped, with volatile higher in two of six, so the chapter states volatile\n  does not help rather than claiming it is worse.\n- A reader spinning on a non-volatile flag never observed the write --\n  323,522,386 spins, exiting only on a 3s cap -- while the volatile version\n  exited in 0 ms. Identical on every run.\n- 63 ms synchronized vs 50 ms AtomicInteger, reported but explicitly not used as\n  the basis for the recommendation.\n\nOne demo defect was found and fixed rather than shipped: the first interleaving\ndemo started threads one at a time and printed 0 1 2 3 4 5 on all four runs,\nbecause each task finished before the next thread was created. It would have\ndemonstrated determinism under text asserting non-determinism. Rewritten with a\nCountDownLatch start gate, it now produces a different order every run. The\nclaim was fixed by fixing the demo.\n\nA published five-run range was also widened after a later run landed outside it.\n\nA stale claim in question-bank/02-java-concurrency.md -- that concurrency is\n\"inherently a Mid+ topic in this repository''s own target-level design\" -- was\nrevisited rather than defended, and the note records the change and reasoning.\n\nShips with its Interview Answer Framework inside section 15 from the start,\nfollowing the same-date scaffold work. 30-second answer verified at 55 words,\n24 seconds.\n\nCompanions: 1 cheat sheet, 1 deck (5 cards). Question bank 72->80, 02-java\ndomain total reconciled 281->289. Both indexes and both changelogs updated.\nvalidate.py: errors 0, warnings 13 (unchanged baseline). Anchor check: 233\nanchored links, 0 bad.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"62fa706b482b758c893b6740c37007438e93a7e8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/git/trees/62fa706b482b758c893b6740c37007438e93a7e8"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/git/commits/0520b5576f8a4a227fc79c0b04fe4cba87f5fa2c","verification":{"payload":"tree 62fa706b482b758c893b6740c37007438e93a7e8\nparent 73ccdd8ed81615823f446c34a923a2f4d829f5e8\nauthor Juan Antonio Melendres Villa <juan.mele97@gmail.com> 1790796225 -0600\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790796225 -0600\n\nfeat: add Java concurrency fundamentals on-ramp (T-2214) (#199)\n\nThird and final item from the queue following the user''s lost interview, where\nconcurrency was one of the topics they knew but could not explain.\n\nThe audit result was mixed rather than flattering. The concepts were already\ncovered -- the Phase 5 L1/L2 retrofit gave the existing chapters genuine\nplain-language Foundation sections, and claiming the material was missing would\nhave been wrong. Two things were genuinely absent, both verified by count:\n02-java/concurrency was the only Java subdomain without a Junior Fundamentals\non-ramp, and `new Thread(` had zero occurrences anywhere in 02-java. The\nrepository never showed how to start a thread.\n\nNew chapter assembles what fifteen Senior/Staff chapters cover separately and\nhands off to each by name. Measured, pure JDK, OpenJDK 21.0.12, 10 cores:\n\n- 8 threads x 100,000 increments on a shared int produced 229,765 of 800,000 --\n  roughly 70-85% lost across six runs, with nothing thrown.\n- The same workload with a volatile counter produced 177,394. Across six runs\n  the plain range (132,587-229,765) and volatile range (135,457-195,568)\n  overlapped, with volatile higher in two of six, so the chapter states volatile\n  does not help rather than claiming it is worse.\n- A reader spinning on a non-volatile flag never observed the write --\n  323,522,386 spins, exiting only on a 3s cap -- while the volatile version\n  exited in 0 ms. Identical on every run.\n- 63 ms synchronized vs 50 ms AtomicInteger, reported but explicitly not used as\n  the basis for the recommendation.\n\nOne demo defect was found and fixed rather than shipped: the first interleaving\ndemo started threads one at a time and printed 0 1 2 3 4 5 on all four runs,\nbecause each task finished before the next thread was created. It would have\ndemonstrated determinism under text asserting non-determinism. Rewritten with a\nCountDownLatch start gate, it now produces a different order every run. The\nclaim was fixed by fixing the demo.\n\nA published five-run range was also widened after a later run landed outside it.\n\nA stale claim in question-bank/02-java-concurrency.md -- that concurrency is\n\"inherently a Mid+ topic in this repository''s own target-level design\" -- was\nrevisited rather than defended, and the note records the change and reasoning.\n\nShips with its Interview Answer Framework inside section 15 from the start,\nfollowing the same-date scaffold work. 30-second answer verified at 55 words,\n24 seconds.\n\nCompanions: 1 cheat sheet, 1 deck (5 cards). Question bank 72->80, 02-java\ndomain total reconciled 281->289. Both indexes and both changelogs updated.\nvalidate.py: errors 0, warnings 13 (unchanged baseline). Anchor check: 233\nanchored links, 0 bad.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqvWHBCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAARRUQAKjQkWRkd6zA5WGSFk9eeBmb\nwDfdX15Whotd1uv2Z12rZvLWY/2TCNBLr8CDp+seAYFMXRHOdqbE3Pe9oi1vt9xJ\nAbWagV9SR0ZOO0/DP3HLKS40dcqJJZf33ma9RmrdrFc8a/vg2za94USUa8SrpafF\nr3uYfy0vx/CLWOhsiX9Yt0I26IMeVzTqA58mCw6WYo+hbD5K9C/k8U8sKa4MAXRO\nL/aYtuS5lYxAnfUB3f4RSvv0HHXHzMVXydzJcNa1TIbtXPGuFeBua5V7gDBfJNKB\nU+cpMe+89nlGjOtsKXSobEOUF84mw+cVi+F2af8kaGi9TMoFfbEiIwYaIN5mfZnO\nq0Da3ZXh5GiqAlm0sPbQ9qkGYBD+zf/IIQGDvqAlAKIUXpg07zDDdpa5YlZzs+t9\nUXaHQyDgPmXUh9a+HxrZyiM33Qxj23DfGVPAdojNxzEAYB5/snDGKXIqXFJU8A4p\ngYDThqWVMgi0E92zZom6qf7gNTN5hvFAnTn/I7oZSCLdFC4D8EguLOJsQPinOd0G\nkFoSxyOSS7gzIlfSBTcKDQzIlreBEm/RhOGHRbbx847hw/KpAN0cKTAOib/4Bn3F\nA1aFpfPEP1M9zLWFeQlLtiZDzgZVhOl8PEFKpJF62RstjkB0WX1cgVdcsQh7rm94\nVlKQ3XdcyK7p9IGzY6QM\n=Wte6\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-30T19:23:46Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commit/0520b5576f8a4a227fc79c0b04fe4cba87f5fa2c","node_id":"C_kwDOTnPgT9oAKDA1MjBiNTU3NmY4YTRhMjI3ZmM3OWMwYjA0ZmU0Y2JhODdmNWZhMmM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commit/73ccdd8ed81615823f446c34a923a2f4d829f5e8","sha":"73ccdd8ed81615823f446c34a923a2f4d829f5e8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commits/73ccdd8ed81615823f446c34a923a2f4d829f5e8"}],"sha":"0520b5576f8a4a227fc79c0b04fe4cba87f5fa2c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commits/0520b5576f8a4a227fc79c0b04fe4cba87f5fa2c"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/13798190?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/JuanMelendres","id":13798190,"login":"JuanMelendres","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjEzNzk4MTkw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commits/73ccdd8ed81615823f446c34a923a2f4d829f5e8/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-30T19:04:29Z","email":"juan.mele97@gmail.com","name":"Juan Antonio Melendres Villa"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-30T19:04:29Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat: add layered answer scaffold to the 13 Junior Fundamentals chapters (#198)\n\nFollow-up to the same-date delivery work, which found 65 chapters carrying\ninterview questions with no 30-second/2-minute/10-minute scaffold, and\nestablished that all 65 are syllabus-topic while zero handbook-chapter lacks it\n-- a consequence of the 20-section Topic Specification folding \"Interview\nQuestions\" and \"Interview Answer Framework\" into its section 15, not neglect.\n\nCloses that gap for the 13 Junior Fundamentals chapters (T-2201 through\nT-2213), which cover exactly what a screening round asks and were the only\nchapters in their own domains lacking the scaffold -- 02-java/collections had\n10 of 11, 06-databases 18 of 19, 07-api-design 8 of 9.\n\nEach gained an Interview Answer Framework subsection inside section 15, which\npreserves the 20-section numbering and leaves the #15-interview-questions\nanchor the question banks link to untouched.\n\nEvery 30-second answer was measured rather than estimated, using the\naudit_answer_delivery_length.py script written the same day: 13 of 13 within\nthe 70-word budget, median 26 seconds, tighter than the repository''s existing\nmedian of 30. One draft came in at 72 words and was trimmed rather than shipped\nover budget.\n\nThe 10-minute layers are labelled as outlines inline, with a pointer to the\ndelivery chapter, rather than implying they are scripts -- the same honesty\nthat chapter argues for, applied to its own output.\n\nContent is grounded per chapter, not templated. Cheat sheets are deliberately\nunchanged: they already carry a compressed 30-sec line, and the established\npattern across the other 220 chapters is exactly that pair, so a third copy\nwould have been duplication. No question-bank edits, because no questions were\nadded.\n\nAlso fixes 5 pre-existing broken anchors in spring-and-spring-boot-fundamentals\nthat pointed at #core-concepts and #internal-implementation on a syllabus-topic\nchapter whose real anchors are #4-core-concepts-l2 and\n#5-how-it-works-internally-l3. Out of scope, recorded as such in both\nchangelogs.\n\nvalidate.py: errors 0, warnings 13 (unchanged baseline). Anchor check: 255\nanchored links across the changed files, 0 bad.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"c76fe735dd88d29fa807eb35e6c63f1058760723","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/git/trees/c76fe735dd88d29fa807eb35e6c63f1058760723"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/git/commits/73ccdd8ed81615823f446c34a923a2f4d829f5e8","verification":{"payload":"tree c76fe735dd88d29fa807eb35e6c63f1058760723\nparent 4933ccc57217de3fcbf43bda51805c2eaa08ea1f\nauthor Juan Antonio Melendres Villa <juan.mele97@gmail.com> 1790795069 -0600\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790795069 -0600\n\nfeat: add layered answer scaffold to the 13 Junior Fundamentals chapters (#198)\n\nFollow-up to the same-date delivery work, which found 65 chapters carrying\ninterview questions with no 30-second/2-minute/10-minute scaffold, and\nestablished that all 65 are syllabus-topic while zero handbook-chapter lacks it\n-- a consequence of the 20-section Topic Specification folding \"Interview\nQuestions\" and \"Interview Answer Framework\" into its section 15, not neglect.\n\nCloses that gap for the 13 Junior Fundamentals chapters (T-2201 through\nT-2213), which cover exactly what a screening round asks and were the only\nchapters in their own domains lacking the scaffold -- 02-java/collections had\n10 of 11, 06-databases 18 of 19, 07-api-design 8 of 9.\n\nEach gained an Interview Answer Framework subsection inside section 15, which\npreserves the 20-section numbering and leaves the #15-interview-questions\nanchor the question banks link to untouched.\n\nEvery 30-second answer was measured rather than estimated, using the\naudit_answer_delivery_length.py script written the same day: 13 of 13 within\nthe 70-word budget, median 26 seconds, tighter than the repository''s existing\nmedian of 30. One draft came in at 72 words and was trimmed rather than shipped\nover budget.\n\nThe 10-minute layers are labelled as outlines inline, with a pointer to the\ndelivery chapter, rather than implying they are scripts -- the same honesty\nthat chapter argues for, applied to its own output.\n\nContent is grounded per chapter, not templated. Cheat sheets are deliberately\nunchanged: they already carry a compressed 30-sec line, and the established\npattern across the other 220 chapters is exactly that pair, so a third copy\nwould have been duplication. No question-bank edits, because no questions were\nadded.\n\nAlso fixes 5 pre-existing broken anchors in spring-and-spring-boot-fundamentals\nthat pointed at #core-concepts and #internal-implementation on a syllabus-topic\nchapter whose real anchors are #4-core-concepts-l2 and\n#5-how-it-works-internally-l3. Out of scope, recorded as such in both\nchangelogs.\n\nvalidate.py: errors 0, warnings 13 (unchanged baseline). Anchor check: 255\nanchored links across the changed files, 0 bad.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqvV09CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAGn4QAKgjmEMgMMPCKiceuuqRAGgU\n4BVYyjy+jYYan/u/NVEG+5ncyg0mV0N2trH7jKWSirXO99OI137nbgGSuIf8dkAs\nZmB7257x8wn27G5d9bx9i36lNXY9ebf0ccw0xwmbCyVENwEoY22PqTkUL9LqQGiA\nMweREaDDH/TQfLtJ3eDP5A4xZqOCepR7PSHY0N3fWTA4bsxUdDoYTcHdaZnKNTwE\nA0YaVA50lwJxxddKy62K7MIZ9JwuM2mPjPCAKPrV2k5JAKTb7pBZPoTAj3/23iy0\n9EFXgpoiADnps0V3w2qwBkjxyaMm/g43hpAPfBnoykOijRUgImDLaVUEMTNqxH4C\ntpUixTOmncEZorYo6w44XRBcGPrh6IY05djPL/YJzO02w06X9h3vXMmO3ItcV6l4\njHXvWZyqQP0WhxOhks5mdu124HC8mwWhCDOk0KKJPTWsF1jJgzBW34dUVX8nMsaN\nmrp8M74Dust0wIk3pd0igRiHf3+vzl++sR637lTmYQevRntxCyYScI0rQAKo7GK4\n9MDjmn76TXlkWFYimkYo0ZlASexJc02OVBUHeMLme2Q4xTrUkZiguuZmIkCohKFg\nQoMn7yjH+YqPteuxqKp6njRBJJ9bfW6V2gQyi02jEGnmBkWttuN5igObmPAW6h+o\nx2uWpioLKet96cYrAF/R\n=QW1N\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-30T19:04:29Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commit/73ccdd8ed81615823f446c34a923a2f4d829f5e8","node_id":"C_kwDOTnPgT9oAKDczY2NkZDhlZDgxNjE1ODIzZjQ0NmMzNGE5MjNhMmY0ZDgyOWY1ZTg","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commit/4933ccc57217de3fcbf43bda51805c2eaa08ea1f","sha":"4933ccc57217de3fcbf43bda51805c2eaa08ea1f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commits/4933ccc57217de3fcbf43bda51805c2eaa08ea1f"}],"sha":"73ccdd8ed81615823f446c34a923a2f4d829f5e8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commits/73ccdd8ed81615823f446c34a923a2f4d829f5e8"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/13798190?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/JuanMelendres","id":13798190,"login":"JuanMelendres","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjEzNzk4MTkw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commits/4933ccc57217de3fcbf43bda51805c2eaa08ea1f/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-30T18:21:27Z","email":"juan.mele97@gmail.com","name":"Juan Antonio Melendres Villa"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-30T18:21:27Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat: add delivery training -- explaining technical concepts under pressure (T-1606) (#197)\n\nPrompted by a real interview the user lost: asked Java collections, concurrency,\nexceptions, Kafka consumer error handling, and frontend basics, they knew the\nmaterial and could not explain it well.\n\nThe audit does not flatter this repository''s previous work. For five of the six\ntopic areas a calibrated 30-second answer already existed. The material was not\nthe problem. The gap was that nothing here trains delivery and nothing measures\nit.\n\nNew script, scripts/audit_answer_delivery_length.py, measures every layered\nanswer against how long it takes to say, at 140 wpm. Over this repository''s own\n234 answers:\n\n- 30-second answers are well calibrated (median exactly 30s)\n- 2-minute answers are half-written (median 59s, 0.5x)\n- 10-minute deep dives contain a median of 42 seconds of spoken material (0.1x)\n\nThe deep dives are not badly written -- they are outlines (\"Cover, in order: A;\nB; C\"). Reading one aloud produces ~40 seconds of speech with nine minutes left,\nwhich is the mechanical reason an answer opens strongly and thins out under\nfollow-ups. Nothing had said so.\n\nNew chapter covers the five delivery moves (claim first, one concrete instance\nbefore abstraction, signpost parts, name the boundary, stop), analogy\nconstruction on the mechanism-not-category test with unprompted\nboundary-marking, four recovery moves for when a known answer will not come out,\nand recorded self-diagnosis. Worked examples use the user''s six real interview\nquestions verbatim.\n\nNew explanation drill: one concept, three audiences, 60 seconds each, solo and\nrecorded. Assumes the material is known; tests only delivery.\n\nA delivery axis was added to all 17 existing mock rounds. Every rubric scored\ncontent only, so the exact failure this addresses would have scored 4 or 5 and\nbeen invisible to the repository''s own practice loop.\n\nShips deliberately without an executed-code demo -- the subject is\ncommunication, there is nothing to compile, and the provenance line says so\nrather than implying otherwise. The evidence is a committed, re-runnable\nmeasurement instead.\n\nAlso recorded but deliberately not acted on: 65 chapters lack the layered\nanswer scaffold, and all 65 are syllabus-topic while zero handbook-chapter\nlacks it -- a template consequence, not neglect, and a template decision for\nthe user rather than something to change silently.\n\nCompanions: 1 cheat sheet, 1 deck (5 cards). Question bank 33->41. Two indexes,\nthe mock-interviews README, and both changelogs updated. validate.py: errors 0,\nwarnings 13 (unchanged baseline). Anchor check: 200 anchored links, 0 bad.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"0fe30f4ef51b4579ac1d5a7f55d15fa48453ddcd","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/git/trees/0fe30f4ef51b4579ac1d5a7f55d15fa48453ddcd"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/git/commits/4933ccc57217de3fcbf43bda51805c2eaa08ea1f","verification":{"payload":"tree 0fe30f4ef51b4579ac1d5a7f55d15fa48453ddcd\nparent 250b7324b05cbdc8c52394f933064bab49a4335a\nauthor Juan Antonio Melendres Villa <juan.mele97@gmail.com> 1790792487 -0600\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790792487 -0600\n\nfeat: add delivery training -- explaining technical concepts under pressure (T-1606) (#197)\n\nPrompted by a real interview the user lost: asked Java collections, concurrency,\nexceptions, Kafka consumer error handling, and frontend basics, they knew the\nmaterial and could not explain it well.\n\nThe audit does not flatter this repository''s previous work. For five of the six\ntopic areas a calibrated 30-second answer already existed. The material was not\nthe problem. The gap was that nothing here trains delivery and nothing measures\nit.\n\nNew script, scripts/audit_answer_delivery_length.py, measures every layered\nanswer against how long it takes to say, at 140 wpm. Over this repository''s own\n234 answers:\n\n- 30-second answers are well calibrated (median exactly 30s)\n- 2-minute answers are half-written (median 59s, 0.5x)\n- 10-minute deep dives contain a median of 42 seconds of spoken material (0.1x)\n\nThe deep dives are not badly written -- they are outlines (\"Cover, in order: A;\nB; C\"). Reading one aloud produces ~40 seconds of speech with nine minutes left,\nwhich is the mechanical reason an answer opens strongly and thins out under\nfollow-ups. Nothing had said so.\n\nNew chapter covers the five delivery moves (claim first, one concrete instance\nbefore abstraction, signpost parts, name the boundary, stop), analogy\nconstruction on the mechanism-not-category test with unprompted\nboundary-marking, four recovery moves for when a known answer will not come out,\nand recorded self-diagnosis. Worked examples use the user''s six real interview\nquestions verbatim.\n\nNew explanation drill: one concept, three audiences, 60 seconds each, solo and\nrecorded. Assumes the material is known; tests only delivery.\n\nA delivery axis was added to all 17 existing mock rounds. Every rubric scored\ncontent only, so the exact failure this addresses would have scored 4 or 5 and\nbeen invisible to the repository''s own practice loop.\n\nShips deliberately without an executed-code demo -- the subject is\ncommunication, there is nothing to compile, and the provenance line says so\nrather than implying otherwise. The evidence is a committed, re-runnable\nmeasurement instead.\n\nAlso recorded but deliberately not acted on: 65 chapters lack the layered\nanswer scaffold, and all 65 are syllabus-topic while zero handbook-chapter\nlacks it -- a template consequence, not neglect, and a template decision for\nthe user rather than something to change silently.\n\nCompanions: 1 cheat sheet, 1 deck (5 cards). Question bank 33->41. Two indexes,\nthe mock-interviews README, and both changelogs updated. validate.py: errors 0,\nwarnings 13 (unchanged baseline). Anchor check: 200 anchored links, 0 bad.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqvVMnCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAv38QAJZ+1oNqPH4kTrWRL328mHh6\nWrwvTzq/Z2bD2fdgaddoW67ub77Q35OGul1jiwO9nvI0hUVrFQJHfDw2/ScwY9Sp\n6G3Fr6x/JDIeAmbQ87qszgma8ZXqUck2UIZxPIAQcfWinuymg0Y1Mqxrk7YVUZMW\nExqIz5CejvTPo3niriJ1kRwqAyn4Fl246Onwzor1qtgAyxzziTZK9K7uMK+Tz7zp\nAUJe0NkqVXD1HKJxW6PFKy5ah+UOAZnVdwyw88SYTFAEcwdPUqZqqSCDyq1e5TtJ\nOFux7ZjxChbD+1DYBzPnDJ52yrKmq/yRUjTEk2KWmIFSgaTB9UoUIynzVf5G8P2E\nkQs8CxGGfOC6qVAUHott59XNCIII7dWmNKHREtNv7e4okLQNhtOPTmLNuD0/H8TI\nymLTCxM39mD+/FSqvx3AZ4spYkfPnD5AiKNe36MbEHchY7ugrTEngffZUglsPEJl\ngq3hItB4oFNvaNA2GGe5/pyJcceNSKUoUCpl5TGeDyqEB/MRnu0iZihstwLmsloT\nNl9ZJ6xnAfOFDZJLy1UAFKBiFqslbYmq64hRjYqEKgevXxYUIOKajaZ4E3o/FWjI\n6OfuhsqguDPFITXNZqMe3wHAAhQBKuT6mw8aE7evm8uMP5/IsR2HIVqeYkQzoBo2\ne4/vqNT7Nm7vrOEP8tMt\n=GWuL\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-30T18:21:28Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commit/4933ccc57217de3fcbf43bda51805c2eaa08ea1f","node_id":"C_kwDOTnPgT9oAKDQ5MzNjY2M1NzIxN2RlM2ZjYmY0M2JkYTUxODA1YzJlYWEwOGVhMWY","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commit/250b7324b05cbdc8c52394f933064bab49a4335a","sha":"250b7324b05cbdc8c52394f933064bab49a4335a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commits/250b7324b05cbdc8c52394f933064bab49a4335a"}],"sha":"4933ccc57217de3fcbf43bda51805c2eaa08ea1f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commits/4933ccc57217de3fcbf43bda51805c2eaa08ea1f"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/13798190?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/JuanMelendres","id":13798190,"login":"JuanMelendres","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjEzNzk4MTkw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commits/250b7324b05cbdc8c52394f933064bab49a4335a/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-29T21:03:43Z","email":"juan.mele97@gmail.com","name":"Juan Antonio Melendres Villa"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-29T21:03:43Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat: add collector internals to streams-and-collectors (characteristics, combiner lifecycle, catalog) (#195)\n\nAudits a user-supplied \"Collectors Internals\" infographic against real content.\nThe four-part collector model was already present -- a commented Collector.of\nexample, measured toMap duplicate-key behaviour, measured parallel corruption of\na shared ArrayList, and a measured 6.6x parallel slowdown. Three gaps remained,\neach verified by a repository-wide count before writing.\n\nExtends streams-and-collectors.md (1.0 -> 1.1, 3,903 -> 6,374 words) rather than\nsplitting, since the topic already has a canonical home.\n\nMeasured via a Collector instrumented to count every function invocation:\n\n- The combiner is never called in a sequential stream: combiner=0 sequentially\n  vs combiner=63 in parallel over 1,000 elements, with 64 containers created on\n  a 10-core machine. A deliberately broken combiner, (a, b) -> a, returned all\n  1,000 elements sequentially and 15 in parallel across three runs, with no\n  exception -- 98.5% silent data loss that no sequential test can detect.\n- Collector.Characteristics had zero occurrences repository-wide.\n  IDENTITY_FINISH genuinely skips the finisher: 1 invocation without the flag,\n  0 with it. The real characteristic sets of nine built-in collectors are read\n  from the JDK rather than described.\n- CONCURRENT is a different execution strategy, not a faster groupingBy.\n  Grouping 10,000 elements into 4 groups in parallel: groupingBy built 67\n  containers and merged 63 times; groupingByConcurrent built 4 and merged 0.\n- Only four Collectors factory methods were mentioned anywhere in the\n  repository. Added a table of 24 run against one shared dataset, covering the\n  13 previously uncovered.\n\nTwo traps surfaced by checking rather than assuming: Collectors.toList() is\nmutable while Stream.toList() and toUnmodifiableList() throw; and toMap''s\ndefault HashMap scrambles insertion order where a LinkedHashMap factory does\nnot.\n\nNew interview question on the combiner''s purpose, answered from the\nmeasurements. Cheat sheet gained a Collector Internals section and a selection\ntable; deck went 3 -> 7 cards. Question bank, domain index, and both changelogs\nupdated. validate.py: errors 0, warnings 13 (unchanged baseline). Anchor check:\n175 anchored links across the changed files, 0 bad.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"c01c208e1e9c7f946d99ef2cf597fd292f449ba8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/git/trees/c01c208e1e9c7f946d99ef2cf597fd292f449ba8"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/git/commits/250b7324b05cbdc8c52394f933064bab49a4335a","verification":{"payload":"tree c01c208e1e9c7f946d99ef2cf597fd292f449ba8\nparent f8f4c3365afdd4db70f6351f3cb1aebd75fb2b65\nauthor Juan Antonio Melendres Villa <juan.mele97@gmail.com> 1790715823 -0600\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790715823 -0600\n\nfeat: add collector internals to streams-and-collectors (characteristics, combiner lifecycle, catalog) (#195)\n\nAudits a user-supplied \"Collectors Internals\" infographic against real content.\nThe four-part collector model was already present -- a commented Collector.of\nexample, measured toMap duplicate-key behaviour, measured parallel corruption of\na shared ArrayList, and a measured 6.6x parallel slowdown. Three gaps remained,\neach verified by a repository-wide count before writing.\n\nExtends streams-and-collectors.md (1.0 -> 1.1, 3,903 -> 6,374 words) rather than\nsplitting, since the topic already has a canonical home.\n\nMeasured via a Collector instrumented to count every function invocation:\n\n- The combiner is never called in a sequential stream: combiner=0 sequentially\n  vs combiner=63 in parallel over 1,000 elements, with 64 containers created on\n  a 10-core machine. A deliberately broken combiner, (a, b) -> a, returned all\n  1,000 elements sequentially and 15 in parallel across three runs, with no\n  exception -- 98.5% silent data loss that no sequential test can detect.\n- Collector.Characteristics had zero occurrences repository-wide.\n  IDENTITY_FINISH genuinely skips the finisher: 1 invocation without the flag,\n  0 with it. The real characteristic sets of nine built-in collectors are read\n  from the JDK rather than described.\n- CONCURRENT is a different execution strategy, not a faster groupingBy.\n  Grouping 10,000 elements into 4 groups in parallel: groupingBy built 67\n  containers and merged 63 times; groupingByConcurrent built 4 and merged 0.\n- Only four Collectors factory methods were mentioned anywhere in the\n  repository. Added a table of 24 run against one shared dataset, covering the\n  13 previously uncovered.\n\nTwo traps surfaced by checking rather than assuming: Collectors.toList() is\nmutable while Stream.toList() and toUnmodifiableList() throw; and toMap''s\ndefault HashMap scrambles insertion order where a LinkedHashMap factory does\nnot.\n\nNew interview question on the combiner''s purpose, answered from the\nmeasurements. Cheat sheet gained a Collector Internals section and a selection\ntable; deck went 3 -> 7 cards. Question bank, domain index, and both changelogs\nupdated. validate.py: errors 0, warnings 13 (unchanged baseline). Anchor check:\n175 anchored links across the changed files, 0 bad.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqvCevCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAk5EQAJ6GD8J/+GDK9BNgIzOoyXVP\nDq7NzY6qsTWBqcWsp0M4WUOzW3GxAHkzFIJatn8ChBPn4ctkeyblsNl0buMF3rgq\nzCHES6dZ+yA19yo643xcTzzXk8qpp2H2DjBvc9xUYCvvpg6HKR3OIDQa8jBfjK1i\nYzV0k26XJCSsWkuaMqiSNP9QNV2w/nXvFdnaHjTp1culvGOvLsLuR+LmRu2UKDTd\nBv22bGZ7ccg36dTyfcIl864fGWsN+gXcYCvZC7t4BKRKJgLOXmX0Qnr96gEuIvih\n7/24+z2XN/dfA8DbkZ2iZwKoQXY7JjCBDIrjt9w4wKmPWXbiz75euGuxORfAs0Hl\n0K9dOkldHtD82sqqlsgQK2j/qIJ8t/MQcDWWDMF0fAcMlM1/MTcna4nK25IvCLbu\nYVK45eJdhYa1g37o2WSxRQzULUgzj+NVm+kgsVMQXVbgd4DXQKWTiDJVMjTxE4Ot\nobW1uC6f0gOPzldII1KOrEnkCEKeZWAkYPV5NkwayuG8ydH+pncV7jgu7wYlswId\nTV1rKnALkgTrWLoM7o9c4cJZlhY2RgIjtd0FmS1iUL6T2R27CAorW3u9CBSA7R2+\n+Woj3LEI8amqlDYSVplHZAmmLk2RMwnTk2fMYNcIify4Zvh/DTD++XTDM8d9y7/I\nwuIwqfHLd7u6IdCNUais\n=cbaA\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-29T21:03:43Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commit/250b7324b05cbdc8c52394f933064bab49a4335a","node_id":"C_kwDOTnPgT9oAKDI1MGI3MzI0YjA1Y2JkYzhjNTIzOTRmOTMzMDY0YmFiNDlhNDMzNWE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commit/f8f4c3365afdd4db70f6351f3cb1aebd75fb2b65","sha":"f8f4c3365afdd4db70f6351f3cb1aebd75fb2b65","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commits/f8f4c3365afdd4db70f6351f3cb1aebd75fb2b65"}],"sha":"250b7324b05cbdc8c52394f933064bab49a4335a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commits/250b7324b05cbdc8c52394f933064bab49a4335a"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/13798190?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/JuanMelendres","id":13798190,"login":"JuanMelendres","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjEzNzk4MTkw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commits/f8f4c3365afdd4db70f6351f3cb1aebd75fb2b65/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-29T20:35:04Z","email":"juan.mele97@gmail.com","name":"Juan Antonio Melendres Villa"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-29T20:35:04Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat: add batch and scheduled job design chapter (T-2443) (#196)\n\nFound by a fresh topic-coverage scan rather than supplied material. A\nsystematic sweep of common backend interview topics against real repository\ncontent scored well almost everywhere; batch and scheduled job design came back\nat effectively zero. Spring Batch, ShedLock, Quartz, long-running job, and\nre-runnable all returned zero files, and the classic \"your scheduled job runs\non every replica\" failure appeared nowhere -- every `every instance` match was\nabout Java''s static keyword or heap-dump retention.\n\nThe pieces existed scattered (@Scheduled thread mechanics, JDBC batching,\nidempotency, the migration backfill) but no chapter owned the job itself.\n\nNew chapter organised around four decisions: who runs it, how often it commits,\nwhat happens after a crash, what happens to a bad record.\n\nMeasured against a real H2 2.3.232 database, with correctness counted from a\nside-effect table rather than from the job''s own reporting:\n\n- Three instances against 1,000 invoices produced 3,000 side effects from 3\n  workers -- every record processed three times, no error, all three reporting\n  success. Lock-guarded: 1,000 rows, 1 worker. The fix is one conditional\n  UPDATE, and the chapter refuses to present it without the lease expiry, since\n  a lock with no timeout converts duplicate execution into the job never\n  running again.\n- Chunk size is a durability budget, not a performance setting: 148 ms at one\n  row per commit versus 16 ms at 10,000 (9.25x), with worst-case loss going\n  from 0 rows to 9,999 -- exactly one chunk by construction.\n- The checkpoint must commit in the same transaction as the work. A crash at\n  60%: single-transaction committed zero and needed 1,599 units of work to\n  commit 1,000; chunked committed 500, resumed at 501, wasted exactly 99. The\n  single-transaction version is stated as wasteful and fragile, not incorrect.\n- Fail-fast stopped at record 500 leaving 500 unprocessed and was loud; skip\n  processed 999 and reported success with the dropped record existing only as a\n  counter. Skip is safe only when the count is alerted and the record is\n  replayable.\n\nAlso covers restartable versus idempotent with the \"run it twice\" test, and\nstates honestly what Spring Batch adds (the JobRepository) and costs.\n\nCompanions: 1 cheat sheet, 1 deck (5 cards). Question bank 11-system-design\n50->58. Both indexes and both changelogs updated. validate.py: errors 0,\nwarnings 13 (unchanged baseline). Anchor check: 94 anchored links across the\nchanged files, 0 bad.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"4e6fafe7af57b229cdccb21fe98bd54ad2af038d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/git/trees/4e6fafe7af57b229cdccb21fe98bd54ad2af038d"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/git/commits/f8f4c3365afdd4db70f6351f3cb1aebd75fb2b65","verification":{"payload":"tree 4e6fafe7af57b229cdccb21fe98bd54ad2af038d\nparent 01a07ef93b660649ad04d281329a90bc799917c1\nauthor Juan Antonio Melendres Villa <juan.mele97@gmail.com> 1790714104 -0600\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790714104 -0600\n\nfeat: add batch and scheduled job design chapter (T-2443) (#196)\n\nFound by a fresh topic-coverage scan rather than supplied material. A\nsystematic sweep of common backend interview topics against real repository\ncontent scored well almost everywhere; batch and scheduled job design came back\nat effectively zero. Spring Batch, ShedLock, Quartz, long-running job, and\nre-runnable all returned zero files, and the classic \"your scheduled job runs\non every replica\" failure appeared nowhere -- every `every instance` match was\nabout Java''s static keyword or heap-dump retention.\n\nThe pieces existed scattered (@Scheduled thread mechanics, JDBC batching,\nidempotency, the migration backfill) but no chapter owned the job itself.\n\nNew chapter organised around four decisions: who runs it, how often it commits,\nwhat happens after a crash, what happens to a bad record.\n\nMeasured against a real H2 2.3.232 database, with correctness counted from a\nside-effect table rather than from the job''s own reporting:\n\n- Three instances against 1,000 invoices produced 3,000 side effects from 3\n  workers -- every record processed three times, no error, all three reporting\n  success. Lock-guarded: 1,000 rows, 1 worker. The fix is one conditional\n  UPDATE, and the chapter refuses to present it without the lease expiry, since\n  a lock with no timeout converts duplicate execution into the job never\n  running again.\n- Chunk size is a durability budget, not a performance setting: 148 ms at one\n  row per commit versus 16 ms at 10,000 (9.25x), with worst-case loss going\n  from 0 rows to 9,999 -- exactly one chunk by construction.\n- The checkpoint must commit in the same transaction as the work. A crash at\n  60%: single-transaction committed zero and needed 1,599 units of work to\n  commit 1,000; chunked committed 500, resumed at 501, wasted exactly 99. The\n  single-transaction version is stated as wasteful and fragile, not incorrect.\n- Fail-fast stopped at record 500 leaving 500 unprocessed and was loud; skip\n  processed 999 and reported success with the dropped record existing only as a\n  counter. Skip is safe only when the count is alerted and the record is\n  replayable.\n\nAlso covers restartable versus idempotent with the \"run it twice\" test, and\nstates honestly what Spring Batch adds (the JobRepository) and costs.\n\nCompanions: 1 cheat sheet, 1 deck (5 cards). Question bank 11-system-design\n50->58. Both indexes and both changelogs updated. validate.py: errors 0,\nwarnings 13 (unchanged baseline). Anchor check: 94 anchored links across the\nchanged files, 0 bad.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqvCD4CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAlXwQAKV7zVlFqG7ZTWhiQo8ZkAgh\naF46j3Q9Q0WDHWhimXB4GW5PsRr0eISC3/BqgdDeUWUKuUyWsD9fLfvClMmxu6So\nDePPTOh4Xc7qYaPnjFd44ff/b4WCrERvi8N2BLBrs4MVG9wo7KNBPaejGLxDwN/D\n/HcFLGltMo9UkOfqjeqZAVTp2YsmCf5Y/Y3tx4k408eeOB83AfBkWXRCsJh0s9xl\nBGIKypSUk8T3fmYT5RWquN5DQq3lddfGB3KB9xE2xsYm8aH03D1WAgndW8ueS+rY\nqZmJZ8qJdM2TMRiFrPxh+pX68EfL8vi9MY6HdtJXA3TLcoHNgWoYel8tmARo3rIE\nA+yzCTtIZ5++GwZHnohtEnWAerK3AXC9QliFXrnYNzxwWVCiefpsFoUTbH/8K6HV\n/56qs+TStEg1tu94PwV+WDhVzOz8aBq4c0u5T3wqivsL6xdVA67o5SohKjKOZrWr\neDgIryCQqX7+Dbs310QB5pXOiyIVxQVxgEQ3nCr90fS98uTIT42HctCWDtkSLyEI\nQgiZMvKs2r9Jk8VjKTgBlwAeuejOCZm8o2Yj25WvzGNu7kgvAVuCrWLoGcl7AWCs\nNz4RzWNrkWBr5Y690yTVQ4QsQjSX7U6G1pkppnQOmY2g2KaEI67MEl6f3OrNHzxF\npclXtb7n2bUnf/7glxMK\n=SlsO\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-29T20:35:04Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flo