92fe159d8215b7be9946","node_id":"C_kwDOTnPgT9oAKDIzYThjZWUxOTcwM2U1ZjkyNjY4OTJmZTE1OWQ4MjE1YjdiZTk5NDY","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commit/022a48fbc5aaeaa8a8ee2db6614590b31ade466d","sha":"022a48fbc5aaeaa8a8ee2db6614590b31ade466d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commits/022a48fbc5aaeaa8a8ee2db6614590b31ade466d"}],"sha":"23a8cee19703e5f9266892fe159d8215b7be9946","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commits/23a8cee19703e5f9266892fe159d8215b7be9946"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/13798190?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/JuanMelendres","id":13798190,"login":"JuanMelendres","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjEzNzk4MTkw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commits/022a48fbc5aaeaa8a8ee2db6614590b31ade466d/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-29T18:15:16Z","email":"juan.mele97@gmail.com","name":"Juan Antonio Melendres Villa"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-29T18:15:16Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat: add symptom-first production troubleshooting methodology, mock round, and cookbook symptom index (#193)\n\nAudits a user-supplied \"Top 10 toughest Java interview questions\" list of\nproduction-troubleshooting scenarios. Eight of the ten already had a complete\nwalkthrough across 202 production-cookbook entries and 617 chapter Production\nScenarios sections. The real finding was structural: the repository is organised\nentirely by cause, while a scenario interview question is organised by symptom,\nand the cause is exactly what the candidate does not yet know.\n\nNew chapter — Production Troubleshooting Methodology (T-2442): the missing\nsymptom-first entry point, plus full walkthroughs for the two scenarios that\ngenuinely had none (\"slow in production, fine in development\" and \"500s with no\nexception in the logs\").\n\nMeasured findings from two real demos:\n\n- An N+1 and a batched implementation measured 2,809 us vs 510 us at 10 rows and\n  1,283,121 us vs 507 us at 5,000 rows -- a 2531x gap. They have different\n  curves, not different constants, so a dev dataset sits at the one point where\n  they overlap and a faster machine does not close the gap.\n- With a pool of 10, throughput flattened at ~7,600 req/s from concurrency 10\n  and was no better at 200, while p50 held at 1 ms and p99 reached 152 ms. A\n  mean-latency dashboard reports that saturated system as healthy.\n- Four endpoints return byte-identical 500s for three distinguishable reasons.\n  Raising the application package to DEBUG recovered one and only one of them,\n  which is what makes a log-level change a discriminating step rather than a\n  hopeful one. The three differ in whether the evidence survives without a\n  deploy.\n\nNew mock round — Production Debugging Round: the 17th round and the first\norganised by symptom. All ten questions, 60 minutes, candidate/evaluator\nsections separated, a rubric that scores whether the candidate states what each\ncheck eliminates. CLAUDE.md''s Mock Interview Standard has listed this round type\nsince the project started; it was the one never built.\n\nProduction Cookbook gains a symptom-first index: 18 symptoms mapped to candidate\ncauses and representative entries, since the existing index by cause requires\nalready knowing the answer. All 60+ entry links verified to resolve.\n\nCompanions: 1 cheat sheet, 1 deck (7 cards). Question bank, three indexes, and\nboth changelogs updated. validate.py: errors 0, warnings 13 (unchanged\nbaseline). Anchor check: 80 anchored links across the changed files, 0 bad.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"abac7a761b1ab58352dfd20140883366d749644e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/git/trees/abac7a761b1ab58352dfd20140883366d749644e"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/git/commits/022a48fbc5aaeaa8a8ee2db6614590b31ade466d","verification":{"payload":"tree abac7a761b1ab58352dfd20140883366d749644e\nparent 7f7ed4fe6975f0102b29afc82725423fde227172\nauthor Juan Antonio Melendres Villa <juan.mele97@gmail.com> 1790705716 -0600\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790705716 -0600\n\nfeat: add symptom-first production troubleshooting methodology, mock round, and cookbook symptom index (#193)\n\nAudits a user-supplied \"Top 10 toughest Java interview questions\" list of\nproduction-troubleshooting scenarios. Eight of the ten already had a complete\nwalkthrough across 202 production-cookbook entries and 617 chapter Production\nScenarios sections. The real finding was structural: the repository is organised\nentirely by cause, while a scenario interview question is organised by symptom,\nand the cause is exactly what the candidate does not yet know.\n\nNew chapter — Production Troubleshooting Methodology (T-2442): the missing\nsymptom-first entry point, plus full walkthroughs for the two scenarios that\ngenuinely had none (\"slow in production, fine in development\" and \"500s with no\nexception in the logs\").\n\nMeasured findings from two real demos:\n\n- An N+1 and a batched implementation measured 2,809 us vs 510 us at 10 rows and\n  1,283,121 us vs 507 us at 5,000 rows -- a 2531x gap. They have different\n  curves, not different constants, so a dev dataset sits at the one point where\n  they overlap and a faster machine does not close the gap.\n- With a pool of 10, throughput flattened at ~7,600 req/s from concurrency 10\n  and was no better at 200, while p50 held at 1 ms and p99 reached 152 ms. A\n  mean-latency dashboard reports that saturated system as healthy.\n- Four endpoints return byte-identical 500s for three distinguishable reasons.\n  Raising the application package to DEBUG recovered one and only one of them,\n  which is what makes a log-level change a discriminating step rather than a\n  hopeful one. The three differ in whether the evidence survives without a\n  deploy.\n\nNew mock round — Production Debugging Round: the 17th round and the first\norganised by symptom. All ten questions, 60 minutes, candidate/evaluator\nsections separated, a rubric that scores whether the candidate states what each\ncheck eliminates. CLAUDE.md''s Mock Interview Standard has listed this round type\nsince the project started; it was the one never built.\n\nProduction Cookbook gains a symptom-first index: 18 symptoms mapped to candidate\ncauses and representative entries, since the existing index by cause requires\nalready knowing the answer. All 60+ entry links verified to resolve.\n\nCompanions: 1 cheat sheet, 1 deck (7 cards). Question bank, three indexes, and\nboth changelogs updated. validate.py: errors 0, warnings 13 (unchanged\nbaseline). Anchor check: 80 anchored links across the changed files, 0 bad.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqvAA0CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAL9IQAAAggsxjyQV/mUHrxz3IFozs\nMEodoBMxH8vJ+5odGjILqsFLoyWwQ/ZhWAFdXpC9w1DyQb6IKtkbQS03cEvEAuc1\nJ8Sl