wers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/JuanMelendres","id":13798190,"login":"JuanMelendres","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjEzNzk4MTkw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commits/6a206dfedf2736e5728ed83d1d9ea7646ec2395f/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-28T20:43:22Z","email":"juan.mele97@gmail.com","name":"Juan Antonio Melendres Villa"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-28T20:43:22Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat: add real executed visual explanations to 10 algorithms chapters (#160)\n\nA full-repo audit found 10 of the 20 03-data-structures-algorithms chapters\ncarrying zero diagrams -- no Mermaid, no real ASCII -- in the one domain whose\nown Coding Interview Standard in CLAUDE.md requires a \"visual explanation\" per\nalgorithm.\n\nNine chapters gain a real, executed step-by-step trace at the end of Section 5\n(How It Works Internally), where the mechanism is already being explained:\ntwo-pointer walk (LC 11), collapsing search-on-answer window (LC 1011), XOR bit\nlanes (LC 136), a filled edit-distance DP grid (LC 72), greedy level expansion\n(LC 45), the prefix-sum frequency map (LC 560), the sweep-line event list with\nrunning and peak counts (LC 253), merge sort''s real depth-first split/merge\norder, and the monotonic stack''s evolution with each pop''s height x width = area\n(LC 84).\n\nAll nine come from one new program, practice/java/algorithms/visual-traces/\n(OpenJDK 21.0.12, transcript committed), so every number in every diagram is\nregenerable rather than hand-drawn. A hand-drawn DP grid with one wrong cell is\nworse than no diagram, because a reader who cannot reproduce it has no way to\ntell.\n\nThe tenth chapter, coding-interview-pattern-recognition-methodology.md, gains a\nMermaid routing tree keyed on input shape instead -- it teaches a decision, not\nan algorithm, so there is nothing to execute.\n\nAll 10 chapters: version bump and last_updated 2026-09-28. Domain INDEX and both\nchangelogs record the closure and the audit''s remaining queue.\n\nVerification: validate.py errors 0, warnings 13 (unchanged baseline); anchor\ncheck 82 links, 0 bad after fixing one real doubled-hyphen defect it caught.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"3a2fceb5f6f4041e57e89f3c3c45e4fed6a1702a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/git/trees/3a2fceb5f6f4041e57e89f3c3c45e4fed6a1702a"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/git/commits/6a206dfedf2736e5728ed83d1d9ea7646ec2395f","verification":{"payload":"tree 3a2fceb5f6f4041e57e89f3c3c45e4fed6a1702a\nparent 78aea2fc47bc5f46207ac6a62acdb63c854bedb7\nauthor Juan Antonio Melendres Villa <juan.mele97@gmail.com> 1790628202 -0600\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790628202 -0600\n\nfeat: add real executed visual explanations to 10 algorithms chapters (#160)\n\nA full-repo audit found 10 of the 20 03-data-structures-algorithms chapters\ncarrying zero diagrams -- no Mermaid, no real ASCII -- in the one domain whose\nown Coding Interview Standard in CLAUDE.md requires a \"visual explanation\" per\nalgorithm.\n\nNine chapters gain a real, executed step-by-step trace at the end of Section 5\n(How It Works Internally), where the mechanism is already being explained:\ntwo-pointer walk (LC 11), collapsing search-on-answer window (LC 1011), XOR bit\nlanes (LC 136), a filled edit-distance DP grid (LC 72), greedy level expansion\n(LC 45), the prefix-sum frequency map (LC 560), the sweep-line event list with\nrunning and peak counts (LC 253), merge sort''s real depth-first split/merge\norder, and the monotonic stack''s evolution with each pop''s height x width = area\n(LC 84).\n\nAll nine come from one new program, practice/java/algorithms/visual-traces/\n(OpenJDK 21.0.12, transcript committed), so every number in every diagram is\nregenerable rather than hand-drawn. A hand-drawn DP grid with one wrong cell is\nworse than no diagram, because a reader who cannot reproduce it has no way to\ntell.\n\nThe tenth chapter, coding-interview-pattern-recognition-methodology.md, gains a\nMermaid routing tree keyed on input shape instead -- it teaches a decision, not\nan algorithm, so there is nothing to execute.\n\nAll 10 chapters: version bump and last_updated 2026-09-28. Domain INDEX and both\nchangelogs record the closure and the audit''s remaining queue.\n\nVerification: validate.py errors 0, warnings 13 (unchanged baseline); anchor\ncheck 82 links, 0 bad after fixing one real doubled-hyphen defect it caught.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqutFqCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAoj0QAKovZ4U7w0fPqASyhXiqyQ9E\nagYUJPwU7iq6qKj6S0BpMzb+96w3PzNwn1CtGCJNPVVXu4qoXXfXKqzhqVzOcCVw\naVa6xKQtrDaYxCaqtF6iKlwzigAUnk8TZ3llx+uUUrfJ/umDffIY/0OR/du8GITF\nkdDA7i4nvdrjV/bMMOnZXHgJ6J1tIYL1hjXv6ZjnGgm5afcXHNijj4uOTZZN6x6u\nSarLIYI/p3DGuQ621oX8OaAhChtf1TBdNuDk+NnV35KJcrkzxxh159Woot4z/VsQ\nCNnuEDTVoXQzPvWAk6riqDbdryNXQb29lSzsOKxwL5uTs5UCo6DTU4mC6B248LiL\ngXRxvRElAyd4semfn10QbfVaymxuFLnq2R0UbD7Kgv3wggjxRrvsseDzaqDjr2ou\nT7OfXMlM73L+GMK08vxz0gU3dsjGwCD/u4xCss557zJNUP1iKHAZawxFOkFxNXHR\nJBHrk/21K3GizvT+lW9LmWQzKuFTws/274r2EBv5NvboKvEbnIZi8FBI2yKtRlK+\n+9klLa5pR1UKSJNhQhbuJONznhI199TXHmAyZBYQBbRAzeYXadlaVDcB0ABxc81G\nwvg0kiB2tmaGYcZ1Y5ZAmQTwxTeveXywHtYGplPuNCTfmJSPxE/DS4dYxadY9ru3\nlHK3f5UtMCNTF4+Tbeq+\n=lNE3\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-28T20:43:23Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commit/6a206dfedf2736e5728ed83d1d9ea7646ec2395f","node_id":"C_kwDOTnPgT9oAKDZhMjA2ZGZlZGYyNzM2ZTU3MjhlZDgzZDFkOWVhNzY0NmVjMjM5NWY","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commit/78aea2fc47bc5f46207ac6a62acdb63c854bedb7","sha":"78aea2fc47bc5f46207ac6a62acdb63c854bedb7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commits/78aea2fc47bc5f46207ac6a62acdb63c854bedb7"}],"sha":"6a206dfedf2736e5728ed83d1d9ea7646ec2395f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commits/6a206dfedf2736e5728ed83d1d9ea7646ec2395f"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/13798190?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/JuanMelendres","id":13798190,"login":"JuanMelendres","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjEzNzk4MTkw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commits/78aea2fc47bc5f46207ac6a62acdb63c854bedb7/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-28T16:24:27Z","email":"juan.mele97@gmail.com","name":"Juan Antonio Melendres Villa"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-28T16:24:27Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat: close four checklist-audit gaps (nested classes, GoF catalog, varargs, broker selection) (#159)\n\nAudited a self-generated ~70-question Java/backend interview checklist against\nreal repository content. Most items were already covered more deeply than the\nchecklist asks; several near-miss candidates were verified and discarded as\nfalse positives (the Integer -128..127 cache and static-initialization triggers\nare both genuinely covered). Four real gaps remained and are closed here.\n\nNew chapters:\n\n- syllabus/02-java/language-core/nested-and-inner-classes.md (T-2431) --\n  static nested vs. inner vs. local vs. anonymous had no canonical treatment\n  anywhere. Real demos prove the synthetic this$0 field, a real\n  NotSerializableException naming the outer class, and a real 8 MB retention\n  across GC. Two unplanned findings kept as content: javac 21 elides this$0\n  when the inner class never uses its enclosing instance (javap-confirmed), and\n  constant folding of a final String literal can hide the effect entirely.\n\n- syllabus/04-software-design/design-patterns-catalog-beyond-the-core-four.md\n  (T-2432) -- the existing patterns chapter covers only Strategy, Builder,\n  Decorator, and Singleton. Real demos include a JDK dynamic proxy ($Proxy0)\n  retrying a flaky client with no retry code in the implementation. Unplanned\n  finding: a listener unsubscribing itself mid-dispatch threw no\n  ConcurrentModificationException at all and a third listener was silently\n  skipped, because hasNext() is only cursor != size.\n\n- syllabus/09-messaging-event-driven/broker-selection-kafka-rabbitmq-and-sqs.md\n  (T-2433) -- the domain taught Kafka thoroughly but never answered why this\n  broker over another. Comparison and selection guide sourced from official\n  documentation; no performance numbers asserted, since none were measured on\n  the systems compared.\n\nExtended:\n\n- syllabus/02-java/language-core/java-modifiers-and-method-signatures.md\n  (1.1 -> 1.2) -- varargs had zero occurrences repository-wide. Adds Core\n  Concepts and Internals coverage plus Interview Questions 3-5 on varargs, the\n  three-phase overload resolution order, array-versus-varargs spreading,\n  generic varargs heap pollution, covariant returns, and static hiding. Backed\n  by a new real demo and two new deliberately-broken programs producing genuine\n  javac errors.\n\nCompanion deliverables: 3 cheat sheets, 3 flashcard decks (288/288/1015,\ncounted directly against the file system), question-bank entries in\n02-java-language-core.md (+6), 04-software-design.md (+3), and\n09-messaging-event-driven.md (+3), domain INDEX updates for 02-java,\n04-software-design, and 09-messaging-event-driven, the 00-overview INDEX\nchapter counts, and both changelogs.\n\nVerification: validate.py errors 0, warnings 13 (unchanged baseline);\nprogrammatic anchor-resolution check clean across 303 anchored links in every\nfile touched, covering both live anchor styles.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"f5bfd3709e5fb638633c6b2955fd181976df09a0","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/git/trees/f5bfd3709e5fb638633c6b2955fd181976df09a0"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/git/commits/78aea2fc47bc5f46207ac6a62acdb63c854bedb7","verification":{"payload":"tree f5bfd3709e5fb638633c6b2955fd181976df09a0\nparent 497ba3c0517ef2347df6d234d90a2b20e9114672\nauthor Juan Antonio Melendres Villa <juan.mele97@gmail.com> 1790612667 -0600\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790612667 -0600\n\nfeat: close four checklist-audit gaps (nested classes, GoF catalog, varargs, broker selection) (#159)\n\nAudited a self-generated ~70-question Java/backend interview checklist against\nreal repository content. Most items were already covered more deeply than the\nchecklist asks; several near-miss candidates were verified and discarded as\nfalse positives (the Integer -128..127 cache and static-initialization triggers\nare both genuinely covered). Four real gaps remained and are closed here.\n\nNew chapters:\n\n- syllabus/02-java/language-core/nested-and-inner-classes.md (T-2431) --\n  static nested vs. inner vs. local vs. anonymous had no canonical treatment\n  anywhere. Real demos prove the synthetic this$0 field, a real\n  NotSerializableException naming the outer class, and a real 8 MB retention\n  across GC. Two unplanned findings kept as content: javac 21 elides this$0\n  when the inner class never uses its enclosing instance (javap-confirmed), and\n  constant folding of a final String literal can hide the effect entirely.\n\n- syllabus/04-software-design/design-patterns-catalog-beyond-the-core-four.md\n  (T-2432) -- the existing patterns chapter covers only Strategy, Builder,\n  Decorator, and Singleton. Real demos include a JDK dynamic proxy ($Proxy0)\n  retrying a flaky client with no retry code in the implementation. Unplanned\n  finding: a listener unsubscribing itself mid-dispatch threw no\n  ConcurrentModificationException at all and a third listener was silently\n  skipped, because hasNext() is only cursor != size.\n\n- syllabus/09-messaging-event-driven/broker-selection-kafka-rabbitmq-and-sqs.md\n  (T-2433) -- the domain taught Kafka thoroughly but never answered why this\n  broker over another. Comparison and selection guide sourced from official\n  documentation; no performance numbers asserted, since none were measured on\n  the systems compared.\n\nExtended:\n\n- syllabus/02-java/language-core/java-modifiers-and-method-signatures.md\n  (1.1 -> 1.2) -- varargs had zero occurrences repository-wide. Adds Core\n  Concepts and Internals coverage plus Interview Questions 3-5 on varargs, the\n  three-phase overload resolution order, array-versus-varargs spreading,\n  generic varargs heap pollution, covariant returns, and static hiding. Backed\n  by a new real demo and two new deliberately-broken programs producing genuine\n  javac errors.\n\nCompanion deliverables: 3 cheat sheets, 3 flashcard decks (288/288/1015,\ncounted directly against the file system), question-bank entries in\n02-java-language-core.md (+6), 04-software-design.md (+3), and\n09-messaging-event-driven.md (+3), domain INDEX updates for 02-java,\n04-software-design, and 09-messaging-event-driven, the 00-overview INDEX\nchapter counts, and both changelogs.\n\nVerification: validate.py errors 0, warnings 13 (unchanged baseline);\nprogrammatic anchor-resolution check clean across 303 anchored links in every\nfile touched, covering both live anchor styles.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signa