zZhNjJhY2RiNjNjODU0YmVkYjc","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commit/497ba3c0517ef2347df6d234d90a2b20e9114672","sha":"497ba3c0517ef2347df6d234d90a2b20e9114672","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commits/497ba3c0517ef2347df6d234d90a2b20e9114672"}],"sha":"78aea2fc47bc5f46207ac6a62acdb63c854bedb7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commits/78aea2fc47bc5f46207ac6a62acdb63c854bedb7"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/13798190?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/JuanMelendres","id":13798190,"login":"JuanMelendres","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjEzNzk4MTkw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commits/497ba3c0517ef2347df6d234d90a2b20e9114672/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-28T00:18:41Z","email":"juan.mele97@gmail.com","name":"Juan Antonio Melendres Villa"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-28T00:18:41Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat: close repo-wide question-bank completeness gap across 20 domains (#158)\n\nUser asked to verify every previously-checked interview question this\nsession is correctly reflected in syllabus/20-interview-preparation/\nquestion-bank/ and its canonical chapter. A subagent audit widened the\nprior \"stale index\" detector to catch both the plain ## Interview\nQuestions heading and the older numbered ## N. Interview Questions\nheading, and found a much larger gap population than previously known:\n33 canonical chapters across 20 of 22 domains had a complete, real\nInterview Questions section with zero appearance in their domain''s\nquestion-bank index -- the content already existed and was correct,\nthe index simply never picked it up.\n\nDispatched to 4 parallel background subagents (5-6 domains each, no\noverlapping files). All 4 hit a session-wide rate limit mid-run;\nverified via git diff exactly what each had completed versus left\npartial, then finished the remaining work directly: full sections for\n03-data-structures-algorithms, 04-software-design, 05-spring (including\nthe 20-question spring-and-spring-boot-fundamentals.md, plus removing\nits now-stale \"known gap\" disclaimer), 12-security, 13-observability,\n14-devops-containers, 15-cloud, 16-performance-jvm (4 chapters),\n20-interview-preparation, 21-frontend-web-foundations, and\n22-ai-llm-engineering -- plus the missing quick-fire rows and header\ncounts on the partially-completed files (06-databases, 07-api-design,\n08-testing, 09-messaging-event-driven, 10-distributed-systems,\n11-system-design).\n\nEvery entry summarizes that chapter''s own real answer content -- no\ninvented questions. Caught and fixed one real defect before shipping:\nall 20 spring-and-spring-boot-fundamentals.md entries used the wrong\nanchor (#interview-questions instead of that chapter''s actual\n#15-interview-questions numbered heading), found via a repo-wide\nprogrammatic anchor-resolution check written specifically to verify\nthis batch -- confirmed zero broken links across all 26 question-bank\nfiles afterward.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"01e9e64a39cb4ad2beff45a7ea9eee2a219608b6","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/git/trees/01e9e64a39cb4ad2beff45a7ea9eee2a219608b6"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/git/commits/497ba3c0517ef2347df6d234d90a2b20e9114672","verification":{"payload":"tree 01e9e64a39cb4ad2beff45a7ea9eee2a219608b6\nparent 8a230d9630ea54d96e4eec4d12fd62a0caa86790\nauthor Juan Antonio Melendres Villa <juan.mele97@gmail.com> 1790554721 -0600\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790554721 -0600\n\nfeat: close repo-wide question-bank completeness gap across 20 domains (#158)\n\nUser asked to verify every previously-checked interview question this\nsession is correctly reflected in syllabus/20-interview-preparation/\nquestion-bank/ and its canonical chapter. A subagent audit widened the\nprior \"stale index\" detector to catch both the plain ## Interview\nQuestions heading and the older numbered ## N. Interview Questions\nheading, and found a much larger gap population than previously known:\n33 canonical chapters across 20 of 22 domains had a complete, real\nInterview Questions section with zero appearance in their domain''s\nquestion-bank index -- the content already existed and was correct,\nthe index simply never picked it up.\n\nDispatched to 4 parallel background subagents (5-6 domains each, no\noverlapping files). All 4 hit a session-wide rate limit mid-run;\nverified via git diff exactly what each had completed versus left\npartial, then finished the remaining work directly: full sections for\n03-data-structures-algorithms, 04-software-design, 05-spring (including\nthe 20-question spring-and-spring-boot-fundamentals.md, plus removing\nits now-stale \"known gap\" disclaimer), 12-security, 13-observability,\n14-devops-containers, 15-cloud, 16-performance-jvm (4 chapters),\n20-interview-preparation, 21-frontend-web-foundations, and\n22-ai-llm-engineering -- plus the missing quick-fire rows and header\ncounts on the partially-completed files (06-databases, 07-api-design,\n08-testing, 09-messaging-event-driven, 10-distributed-systems,\n11-system-design).\n\nEvery entry summarizes that chapter''s own real answer content -- no\ninvented questions. Caught and fixed one real defect before shipping:\nall 20 spring-and-spring-boot-fundamentals.md entries used the wrong\nanchor (#interview-questions instead of that chapter''s actual\n#15-interview-questions numbered heading), found via a repo-wide\nprogrammatic anchor-resolution check written specifically to verify\nthis batch -- confirmed zero broken links across all 26 question-bank\nfiles afterward.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqubJhCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAtP8QAB/PsKviVH3BFTAVxsJOnF9T\nGQaXRP6bkPdkBvONK9+2SwFES6I/VZZ6QBD4aalQhnO8dUKZXNp4DFYl31jdzELq\nnW/7CwBxqrG7BsL3doivC0Mr0yEHQ39xHoRa4ZXBGou5587J3rY32npDjikzUlEe\nTS3KCfINUg9mmodp5l0J0a9ycjeBAjAu/mH+wsa5nzpEYz7bIKYOoDiTqIhKSzRG\nUEWBvbNYBZv5j+OukPz7JxLmk89JjRhdRQDIVx7z3VHdfXPg5s6nI3T7Q1HTXYJW\n29U0u5t79kinFfQyUU5Ro6Fa+Ey2IYzexmwOSWJkcvlX3910EQnxaxBvvZeDBSiA\nohL66xtzaAEkhCP42us1f6bfvscg4THA9TzMEBtZVHSSGhIAtfdMHC5+qYei6dqk\n0Fxf2KRtLyzp5Fh5x0IWnwx02AogsfN6++X4HL6fTFgU+cTfBHQ1SFyBvZ7cWylO\nLsdI22VLDhGYgA8vtRltV2PU9nQAutPGvCUUwwvjzC33vosMecgHoNCZqoXSq5fc\nVLemWsXYHeP328XrDCk71KP3htkRRDY3M1Bk9xvwfnIZWnXh2pSX5rg4pr//JTW9\nCRXLNC1Y4H5Ex0suVhTBrMS2nbhZXknV29fMNQbqU/5lMPslQDR3982cLWw49p1I\nU1ePBb5fvIUTQjlgNs3O\n=gcCm\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-28T00:18:42Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commit/497ba3c0517ef2347df6d234d90a2b20e9114672","node_id":"C_kwDOTnPgT9oAKDQ5N2JhM2MwNTE3ZWYyMzQ3ZGY2ZDIzNGQ5MGEyYjIwZTkxMTQ2NzI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commit/8a230d9630ea54d96e4eec4d12fd62a0caa86790","sha":"8a230d9630ea54d96e4eec4d12fd62a0caa86790","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commits/8a230d9630ea54d96e4eec4d12fd62a0caa86790"}],"sha":"497ba3c0517ef2347df6d234d90a2b20e9114672","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commits/497ba3c0517ef2347df6d234d90a2b20e9114672"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/13798190?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/JuanMelendres","id":13798190,"login":"JuanMelendres","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjEzNzk4MTkw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/JuanMelendres","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/commits/8a230d9630ea54d96e4eec4d12fd62a0caa86790/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-27T15:28:05Z","email":"juan.mele97@gmail.com","name":"Juan Antonio Melendres Villa"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-27T15:28:05Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat: close clone()/shallow-vs-deep-copy gap in Immutability chapter (#157)\n\nUser shared a \"50 Java Interview Questions for Experienced Developers\"\nchecklist. Audited: overwhelming majority already covered, deeper than\nthe checklist. One real gap confirmed: shallow copy vs. deep copy /\nObject.clone(). immutability-and-defensive-copying.md covers defensive\ncopying (the general fix for leaking mutable state) in real depth, but\nclone()/Cloneable was never explained anywhere -- no coverage of the\nCloneable marker interface, CloneNotSupportedException, or super.clone()''s\ndefault shallow-copy behavior.\n\nAdded a new Core Concepts subsection and Interview Question 3, full\nJunior-through-Staff standard. Real demo (CloneDemo.java) proves: a class\nnot implementing Cloneable throws a real CloneNotSupportedException; a\nshallow clone() shares the exact same List instance as the original\n(confirmed by reference equality); overriding clone() to deep-copy that\nfield fixes it (also confirmed by reference equality).\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"69fb9eef9bd3314284c60dc8ce2c2063cd7d66f7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/git/trees/69fb9eef9bd3314284c60dc8ce2c2063cd7d66f7"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/JuanMelendres/cracking-code-interviews/git/commits/8a230d9630ea54d96e4eec4d12fd62a0caa86790","verification":{"payload":"tree 69fb9eef9bd3314284c60dc8ce2c2063cd7d66f7\nparent b0f07cead7bffd213b2f375a7a543d0e0fdb259f\nauthor Juan Antonio Melendres Villa <juan.mele97@gmail.com> 1790522885 -0600\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790522885 -0600\n\nfeat: close clone()/shallow-vs-deep-copy gap in Immutability chapter (#157)\n\nUser shared a \"50 Java Interview Questions for Experienced Developers\"\nchecklist. Audited: overwhelming majority already covered, deeper than\nthe checklist. One real gap confirmed: shallow copy vs. deep copy /\nObject.clone(). immutability-and-defensive-copying.md covers defensive\ncopying (the general fix for leaking mutable state) in real depth, but\nclone()/Cloneable was never explained anywhere -- no coverage of the\nCloneable marker interface, CloneNotSupportedException, or super.clone()''s\ndefault shallow-copy behavior.\n\nAdded a new Core Concepts subsection and Interview Question 3, full\nJunior-through-Staff standard. Real demo (CloneDemo.java) proves: a class\nnot implementing Cloneable throws a real CloneNotSupportedException; a\nshallow clone() shares the exact same List instance as the original\n(confirmed by reference equality); overriding clone() to deep-copy that\nfield fixes it (also confirmed by reference equality).\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJquTYFCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAnuQQAB/0e1nlU2oP4AZPJiP3A4jg\nLQsCPs2P8CIeSkuffGNAI431J1CIL3cxHPKSGQoU8Bf9PSsy0yjaDhXl+X6UgwM7\nnCsYXASh0HrGxJhaEwTh/wPkEwc30HHfbLXoYipDPQk2qqISw1tiPXqWnjW1+dkJ\nCHANSsO4X0JbOeOh/dzhcin4/c/O4U1PrP/nPycaIxfTJvoXoJhlz+TuwNMHsPna\nYVMPwo6l+IImtnmQvFY3a49bOC6jqWNKlGra5hHLxE1WzXmoh/fPv4al4bEO4+95\n23fNTHBd5ft/XMpREBUtQ856hOhZLPrn6yB6RjqY9gvCnAezMebjONTHoMPlOmSc\nvs7TsUUhhLWUBBIwGmZWuH3G5unHsGOSWhu/ZL3Arew2R0LyUHol6oXcml+aww8M\nR5mIrpE7ElUZxnw7TqEsAUi2T1SDDO